RAGE brief reports contain a statistical summary and comparison between measurements.
We want to test how this code behaves in various scenarios, and for that we need to generate data that we use as input.
However first lets examine the various random number generators that are available in OCaml and their quality.

{1 Random Number Generators }

We are going to use the generated test data to test various statistical functions, therefore we need a RNG with good statistical properties.

{2 Testing the quality of OCaml's RNGs}

So first lets test the quality of the RNG using the {!module:TestU01} battery of tests (also part of `dieharder` test suite).
An OCaml module is available that wraps the testsuite, and executes tests sequentially, which can take a long time.
To speed up testing we can parallelize the test by running each test in a separate process, using the {!module:Parany} module.
The {!module:Testrng} wraps {!module:TestU01} and automatically parallelizes the tests, storing the output in a separate file for each test, and reporting any failures at the end.

OCaml has changed the implementation of its Random module between versions 4 and 5, we test both using the `stdlib-random` library which allows compiling the 2 versions independently of the compiler's version.

The results are in the table below:
{t
  | RNG                                                | Library        | Test duration | Test failures  |
  | :------------------------------------------------  | :------------: | ------------: | -------------: |
  | {!module:Random4}                                  | [stdlib-random]|          868s |            6 ✗ |
  | {!module:Random5}                                  | [stdlib-random]|          675s |            0 ✓ |
  | {!module:Owl_stats_prng} ([SFMT])                  | [owl]          |          637s |            3 ✗ |
  | {!module:Cryptokit.Random.hardware_rng} ([rdrand]) | [cryptokit]    |         1080s |            2 ✗ |
  | {!module:Mirage_crypto_rng.Fortuna}                | [mirage-crypto-rng]|    10631s |            1 ✓ |
}

The failures where p-values were outside of [[0.001, 0.9990]]:
{t
  | RNG       | Test battery  | Test number | Test name      | Parameters  | p-value |
  | :-------: | :-----------: | ----------: | :------------- | :---------- | ------: |
  | [Random4] | Crush         |        [32] | Gap            | [r = 27]    |   [eps] |
  | [Random4] | pseudoDIEHARD |         [7] | DNA            |             | [0.9992]|
  | [Random4] | BigCrush      |        [35] | Gap            | [r = 25]    |    [eps] |
  | [Random4] | BigCrush      |        [61] | WeightDistrib  | [r = 28]    |    [eps] |
  | [Random4] | BigCrush      |        [64] | WeightDistrib  | [r = 26]    |    [eps] |
  | [Random4] | BigCrush      |        [98] | HammingIndep   |[L=300, r=26]|    [eps] |
  | [SFMT]    | pseudoDIEHARD |         [6] | OQSO           |             |  [0.990] |
  | [SFMT]    | BigCrush      |        [80] | LinearComp     | [r=0]       | [1-eps1] |
  | [SFMT]    | BigCrush      |        [81] | LinearComp     | [r=29]      | [1-eps1] |
  | [Fortuna] | BigCrush      |        [61] | WeightDistrib  | [r=28]      | [5.7e-4] |
  | [rdrand]  | BlockAlphaBit |         [9] | RandomWalk1    | [L = 320]   | [0.9999] |
  | [rdrand]  | BigCrush      |        [11] | CollisionOver  | [t = 21]    | [0.99999]|
 }

Note that a failure is not necessarily a bug in the RNG (even a perfectly random source is expected to fail the tests [~0.2%] of the time when the p-value is [0.001]), which is why the failing tests can be repeated.
However a failure with a p-value near [epsilon] is unlikely to go away upon repetition.

[SFMT] uses [sfmt_genrand_real1], which generates [double] with only 32-bit of precision, [sfmt_genrand_res53] would generate them with [53] bits of precision, but making that change doesn't fix the [LinearComp] failures, and switching to [dSFMT] instead of [SFMT] doesn't help. (The failures with a p-value of ~0.999 could be rerun, but those with a p-value of [eps] or [1-eps1] would almost always fail).

{!module:Random5} (the [Random] module in OCaml version 5) passed all tests, and is quicker than the OCaml 4 version too.

We'll use {!module:Random5} as our RNG.

{2 Generating test data}

We want to generate random numbers from a normal distribution, such that the 95% confidence interval of the mean will be very small, suitable for testing the accuracy of algorithms.
Lets pick [Float.epsilon *. 2. |> sqrt |> sqrt] as the target.

{@ocaml[
  # let sigma =
    let desired_accuracy = Float.epsilon *. 2. |> sqrt |> sqrt in
    let sqrt_n = sqrt (Array.length Datagen.Uniform.fixed |> float) in
    let mu = 1. in
    let alpha = 0.05 in
    (* gaussian_isf * sigma / sqrt(n) = desired_accuracy *)
    sqrt_n *. desired_accuracy /. (Owl.Stats.gaussian_isf ~mu ~sigma:1. (alpha /. 2.));;
  val sigma : float = 0.00627737168498374368
]}

We also want to avoid rounding errors, so we pick [2^n-1] as the size of the generated data ([n=16]),
and use a power of 2 for [sigma < sigma_max], which is [0x1p-7].

We can now view some statistics about the generated data:
{@ocaml[
  # let t95 = Owl.Stats.gaussian_isf ~mu:0. ~sigma:1. 0.025
  val t95 : float = 1.95996398454005449
  # let ci95 ~mean ~sem =
      mean -. t95 *. sem, mean +. t95 *. sem;;
  val ci95 : mean:float -> sem:float -> float * float = <fun>
  # let summarize data =
    let open Owl in
    let lo, hi = Stats.tukey_fences data in
    let mean = Stats.mean data in
    let sem = Stats.sem ~mean data in
    let lo95, hi95 = ci95 ~mean ~sem in
    [ "Mean", mean
    ; "Standard deviation", Stats.std ~mean data
    ; "Standard error of mean", sem
    ; "Skewness", Stats.skew ~mean data
    ; "Kurtosis", Stats.kurtosis ~mean data
    ; "Median", Stats.median data
    ; "Autocorrelation (lag=1)", Stats.autocorrelation data
    ] |> List.iter (fun (desc, v) -> Printf.printf "%s: %g\n" desc v);
    [ "Outlier boundary", lo, hi
    ; "CI 95%", lo95, hi95
    ; "Min, Max", Stats.min data, Stats.max data
    ] |> List.iter (fun (desc, v1, v2) -> Printf.printf "%s: [%g, %g], width = %g\n" desc v1 v2 (abs_float (v1 -. v2)));
    (* check skewness and kurtosis to see whether the data is from a normal distribution,
       only works well for n > 2000 *)
    Printf.printf "Normal distribution: %s\n" (if (Stats.jb_test data).reject then "reject" else "accept")
    ;;
  val summarize : float array -> unit = <fun>
  # summarize Datagen.Uniform.fixed;;
  Mean: 0.5
  Standard deviation: 0.288666
  Standard error of mean: 0.00225527
  Skewness: 5.3403e-17
  Kurtosis: 1.79978
  Median: 0.5
  Autocorrelation (lag=1): 0.995727
  Outlier boundary: [-0.499878, 1.49988], width = 1.99976
  CI 95%: [0.49558, 0.50442], width = 0.00884051
  Min, Max: [6.10352e-05, 0.999939], width = 0.999878
  Normal distribution: reject
  - : unit = ()
  # summarize Datagen.Normal.fixed_accurate;;
  Mean: 1
  Standard deviation: 0.00780863
  Standard error of mean: 6.10067e-05
  Skewness: -8.57629e-14
  Kurtosis: 2.98564
  Median: 1
  Autocorrelation (lag=1): 0.98864
  Outlier boundary: [0.978925, 1.02107], width = 0.0421496
  CI 95%: [0.99988, 1.00012], width = 0.000239142
  Min, Max: [0.969985, 1.03002], width = 0.0600302
  Normal distribution: accept
  - : unit = ()
  # summarize (Datagen.Normal.random_accurate ());;
  Mean: 1
  Standard deviation: 0.00776779
  Standard error of mean: 6.06877e-05
  Skewness: 0.00371282
  Kurtosis: 3.01561
  Median: 0.99999
  Autocorrelation (lag=1): 0.00449697
  Outlier boundary: [0.979166, 1.02073], width = 0.041565
  CI 95%: [0.999881, 1.00012], width = 0.000237891
  Min, Max: [0.96978, 1.03016], width = 0.0603756
  Normal distribution: accept
  - : unit = ()
  # Printf.printf "Ideal CI95%% width = %g\n" (2. *. t95 *. 0x1p-7 /. sqrt (Array.length Datagen.Normal.fixed_accurate |> float));;
  Ideal CI95% width = 0.000239261
  - : unit = ()
  # summarize (Datagen.Normal.fixed2);;
  Mean: 2
  Standard deviation: 0.199901
  Standard error of mean: 0.00156177
  Skewness: -4.06062e-17
  Kurtosis: 2.98564
  Median: 2
  Autocorrelation (lag=1): 0.98864
  Outlier boundary: [1.46049, 2.53951], width = 1.07903
  CI 95%: [1.99694, 2.00306], width = 0.00612204
  Min, Max: [1.23161, 2.76839], width = 1.53677
  Normal distribution: accept
  - : unit = ()
  # summarize (Datagen.Normal.random2 ());;
  Mean: 2.00003
  Standard deviation: 0.200868
  Standard error of mean: 0.00156933
  Skewness: -0.0401492
  Kurtosis: 2.96258
  Median: 2.00097
  Autocorrelation (lag=1): -0.0116703
  Outlier boundary: [1.45734, 2.54295], width = 1.0856
  CI 95%: [1.99696, 2.00311], width = 0.00615165
